179
00:14:16.622 --> 00:14:19.455
Yeah, I am. I am a writer.

180
00:14:24.697 --> 00:14:26.324
Come. I show you.

181
00:14:31.737 --> 00:14:34.297
They come from all over the world every day.

182
00:14:34.373 --> 00:14:35.499
But taking their letters?

183
00:14:35.574 --> 00:14:39.738
Is that not like taking pennies from a wishing well?

184
00:14:39.812 --> 00:14:42.246
How else can we give them answers?

185
00:14:44.049 --> 00:14:46.882
Oh, my God. So, you write all of them back.

186
00:14:47.453 --> 00:14:48.511
Yes.

187
00:14:49.788 --> 00:14:52.313
You're all Juliet? Her secretaries.

188
00:14:56.462 --> 00:14:59.761
Donatella has been married 51 years to the same man.

189
00:15:00.199 --> 00:15:01.894
She handles husband problems.

190
00:15:01.967 --> 00:15:06.028
Husbands are like wine. They take a long time to mature.

191
00:15:07.506 --> 00:15:10.942
Francesca is a nurse. She deals with illness and loss.

192
00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:12.867
And Maria...

193
00:15:12.945 --> 00:15:15.243
Why do you always sigh when you say my name?

194
00:15:15.314 --> 00:15:20.183
She has 12 children, 29 grandchildren, and 16 great-grandchildren.

195
00:15:20.252 --> 00:15:22.982
She writes whatever she likes. And you?

196
00:15:23.055 --> 00:15:26.047
Isabella answers the ones you can barely read.

197
00:15:27.293 --> 00:15:31.457
Yeah, the lovers' quarrels, the breakups, the really tangled hearts.

198
00:15:32.564 --> 00:15:34.361
Someone has to do it.

199
00:15:36.235 --> 00:15:39.102
My mother. Would you like to stay for dinner, Sophie?

200
00:15:48.981 --> 00:15:51.176
Mamma, this is not an insult.

201
00:15:53.519 --> 00:15:58.513
I make a beautiful dish, big enough for the whole town,

202
00:15:59.558 --> 00:16:01.025
and she says no.

203
00:16:01.093 --> 00:16:04.494
I would love to, but I do... I have to go.

204
00:16:04.563 --> 00:16:07.157
This is the problem of the americani.

205
00:16:07.766 --> 00:16:10.394
Always running. Please, Mamma.

206
00:16:11.103 --> 00:16:13.264
I have to meet my fiance

207
00:16:13.639 --> 00:16:14.833
Fiance

208
00:16:23.582 --> 00:16:28.076
At least take some dessert for you and your fiance

209
00:16:48.607 --> 00:16:50.268
Hey, hey, hey, hey.

210
00:16:50.376 --> 00:16:53.174
You know, 120 kilometers each way and I'm still beating you?

211
00:16:53.245 --> 00:16:55.270
Congratulations. What? Yeah. What?

212
00:16:55.347 --> 00:16:57.872
I mean, how was Verona? How was it without me?

213
00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:00.384
Empty? Half-empty? Completely empty?

214
00:17:00.886 --> 00:17:02.217
Half-empty.

215
00:17:02.287 --> 00:17:05.188
I met the Secretaries of Juliet. Juliet?

216
00:17:05.691 --> 00:17:07.556
Like Juliet Capulet? Yeah.

217
00:17:07.626 --> 00:17:11.187
There are these women that call themselves the Secretaries of Juliet,

218
00:17:11.263 --> 00:17:13.857
and they write letters to people that write letters to Juliet.

219
00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:19.102
And then these secretaries, they work for the city of Verona,

220
00:17:19.171 --> 00:17:20.934
and, like, all these people,

221
00:17:21.006 --> 00:17:22.803
these brokenhearted women all over the world,

222
00:17:22.875 --> 00:17:24.968
come and they write letters

223
00:17:25.044 --> 00:17:27.444
and leave them on the wall. What's in the bag?

224
00:17:27.513 --> 00:17:28.980
I don't know.

225
00:17:30.115 --> 00:17:31.343
So, they post these letters.

226
00:17:31.417 --> 00:17:33.647
They post them on the wall in Juliet's courtyard,

227
00:17:33.719 --> 00:17:36.779
and then these secretaries come back with this basket

228
00:17:36.855 --> 00:17:39.585
and they retrieve the letters every day.

229
00:17:40.059 --> 00:17:42.584
And then they write back, as Juliet,

230
00:17:42.661 --> 00:17:44.390
to all the letters that have return addresses on them.

231
00:17:45.531 --> 00:17:47.931
This is their job. I mean, it's so unbelievable.

232
00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:50.059
This is their passion. It's what they do every day.

233
00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:53.568
They literally will work all day, every day. Mmm! Oh, my God!

234
00:17:53.639 --> 00:17:55.283
And they've been doing it for years.

235
00:17:55.284 --> 00:17:56.165
Sophie. Wow. This is incredible!

236
00:17:56.241 --> 00:17:57.640
And they...

237
00:17:57.709 --> 00:17:59.574
This is incredible.

238
00:17:59.645 --> 00:18:02.341
This is incredible. Oh, my God.

239
00:18:02.414 --> 00:18:05.747
This is great. Try this, try this. You have to try this. Oh, my God.

240
00:18:05.818 --> 00:18:08.309
No... Give it a good bite. Come on.

241
00:18:12.257 --> 00:18:14.248
Your Victor is very passionate.

242
00:18:14.326 --> 00:18:16.294
Yes, yes, I know.

243
00:18:16.361 --> 00:18:20.161
But he seems to think that he's Italian since we got here.

244
00:18:20.232 --> 00:18:23.030
And although imitation is, of course, the highest form of flattery,

245
00:18:23.102 --> 00:18:24.399
his imitations are not very good.

246
00:18:24.470 --> 00:18:27.098
Okay, that woman is amazing. I mean, she's incredible.

247
00:18:27.172 --> 00:18:28.662
She's incredible. I think I'm in love.

248
00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:30.640
Absolutely. You're in love with her?

249
00:18:30.709 --> 00:18:33.143
Yeah. Yeah. Really?

250
00:18:33.212 --> 00:18:35.237
Okay. Just say no.

251
00:18:35.314 --> 00:18:36.702
Just say no, and I won't do it, really.

252
00:18:36.703 --> 00:18:38.854
I mean, I'm dying to do it, but I won't.

253
00:18:40.219 --> 00:18:41.584
Okay. What?

254
00:18:42.955 --> 00:18:45.719
Angelina's offered to teach me some secrets today.

255
00:18:45.791 --> 00:18:47.349
Okay. Yeah.

256
00:18:47.426 --> 00:18:49.257
Okay, right now and all day?

257
00:18:49.328 --> 00:18:51.888
Like, right now. That's crazy, right? I mean, now. That's amazing.

258
00:18:51.964 --> 00:18:54.728
I mean, she's making risotto all'Amarone, right? Yeah?

259
00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:56.700
Good risotto. You see?

260
00:18:56.768 --> 00:18:59.204
You see, it's, like, 300 years old or something, her recipe.

261
00:18:59.205 --> 00:18:59.770
It's fantastic.

262
00:18:59.771 --> 00:19:03.502
Okay. That's great. But... So, no Lake Garda then today?

263
00:19:03.575 --> 00:19:04.735
Oh, yeah. Lake Garda...

264
00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:07.938
I mean, Lake Garda's been there for, like, 500,000 years, you know.

265
00:19:08.013 --> 00:19:10.243
And right now, Angelina's offered to help me.

266
00:19:10.315 --> 00:19:12.078
And, you know, I mean, I'm here, we're here, you know.

267
00:19:12.151 --> 00:19:14.119
We're having a good time. You, Paul Newman,

268
00:19:14.186 --> 00:19:17.314
you want to work, you have to watch!

269
00:19:18.157 --> 00:19:21.752
You see? I mean, you see, I became Paul Newman. All right?

270
00:19:22.361 --> 00:19:24.625
Okay, what shall I do? I mean, my hands are tied.

271
00:19:24.696 --> 00:19:25.754
I don't know. I don't know.

272
00:19:25.831 --> 00:19:27.765
You could help us if you like.

273
00:19:27.833 --> 00:19:30.199
Yeah! Yeah. The letter thing, right? Why not?

274
00:19:30.269 --> 00:19:33.204
Yeah, yeah, you write the letters and I'll cook. And later we eat and...

275
00:19:33.272 --> 00:19:34.500
Win-win.

276
00:19:35.607 --> 00:19:37.404
How long have you and Victor been engaged?

277
00:19:37.476 --> 00:19:38.636
Almost a year.

278
00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:42.476
Wow. And may I ask why don't you wear an engagement ring?

279
00:19:42.548 --> 00:19:45.745
Yeah, see, that's... That's me. I insisted.

280
00:19:45.817 --> 00:19:49.651
He was so busy, you know, this whole restaurant and everything.

281
00:19:49.721 --> 00:19:52.246
And I guess I felt it unnecessary.

282
00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.660
Still, a girl should have a ring.

283
00:20:45.043 --> 00:20:47.011
"I didn't go to him, Juliet.

284
00:20:48.146 --> 00:20:50.114
"I didn't go to Lorenzo.

285
00:20:51.016 --> 00:20:53.348
"His eyes were so full of trust.

286
00:20:54.052 --> 00:20:57.021
"I promised I'd meet him to run away together

287
00:20:57.089 --> 00:20:59.182
"because my parents don't approve.

288
00:20:59.258 --> 00:21:03.752
"But instead, I left him waiting for me, below our tree,

289
00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:07.221
"waiting and wondering where I was.

290
00:21:08.367 --> 00:21:09.925
"I'm in Verona now.

291
00:21:10.002 --> 00:21:13.438
"I return to London in the morning, and I'm so afraid.

292
00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:17.673
"Please, Juliet, tell me what I should do.

293
00:21:18.443 --> 00:21:22.504
"My heart is breaking, and I have no one else to turn to.

294
00:21:23.048 --> 00:21:24.572
"Love, Claire. "

295
00:21:24.983 --> 00:21:27.713
And it's been there for all those years?

296
00:21:29.154 --> 00:21:33.784
I think she came back to find her one true love.

297
00:21:34.493 --> 00:21:38.657
And they had 10 children and still make passionate love every night.

298
00:21:40.132 --> 00:21:45.035
Except he got bald and fat and she has to do all the work.

299
00:21:45.103 --> 00:21:46.468
Francesca. Francesca.

300
00:21:46.538 --> 00:21:50.804
And maybe she stayed in London and married a duke

301
00:21:50.876 --> 00:21:53.777
and lived happily ever after, just like me.

302
00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:57.911
But you married an accountant, not a duke. Nobody's perfect.

303
00:21:57.983 --> 00:22:01.077
And I bet when she's making love to that duke...

304
00:22:01.153 --> 00:22:04.418
Yeah? ... she's imagining her first love, Lorenzo.

305
00:22:07.559 --> 00:22:10.221
Isabella, I have to write back.

306
00:22:17.736 --> 00:22:19.328
Then answer her.

307
00:22:46.398 --> 00:22:48.127
Good night, friends.

308
00:23:13.625 --> 00:23:14.990
Good night.

309
00:24:12.451 --> 00:24:13.509
Hey.

310
00:24:14.753 --> 00:24:16.015
Hey.

311
00:24:16.087 --> 00:24:19.022
Sorry I'm late. No, it's fine. No worries.

312
00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:20.785
How was your day? Good? It was...

313
00:24:20.859 --> 00:24:22.190
It was so good. Yeah?

314
00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:23.887
Yeah. Good. I'm glad.

315
00:24:23.962 --> 00:24:25.088
How was Angelina?

316
00:24:25.163 --> 00:24:27.722
Yeah, it was good. I mean, she's amazing, you know.

317
00:24:27.723 --> 00:24:28.793
That woman is incredible.

318
00:24:28.867 --> 00:24:31.927
She's very instinctive, you know. She cooks without recipes.

319
00:24:32.003 --> 00:24:33.061
I mean, she's incredible.

320
00:24:33.138 --> 00:24:34.867
She's invited me to cook again. Really?

321
00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:37.135
Yeah. Yeah, I think I can learn a lot from her.

322
00:24:37.409 --> 00:24:38.603
But...

323
00:24:38.677 --> 00:24:41.305
No, no, you first, you first. Sorry, sorry.

324
00:24:41.379 --> 00:24:44.314
No, just real quick. I just... Mmm-hmm?

325
00:24:44.382 --> 00:24:46.441
I had the most amazing day.

326
00:24:46.518 --> 00:24:49.646
I got to answer a letter that was written 50 years ago.

327
00:24:49.721 --> 00:24:54.055
I know it might not get there, but it just feels so good to write.

328
00:24:54.659 --> 00:24:57.287
Yeah, I think you should spend more time with them.

329
00:24:57.362 --> 00:25:00.422
Why? Because it's your passion, right?

330
00:25:01.466 --> 00:25:03.366
And also because it fits perfectly.

331
00:25:03.435 --> 00:25:06.097
Because I just got off the phone with Signor Morini,

332
00:25:06.171 --> 00:25:08.708
and he's invited me to this amazing wine auction

333
00:25:08.709 --> 00:25:09.938
that's happening in Livorno.

334
00:25:10.008 --> 00:25:12.238
In Livorno? Yeah, yeah, yeah.

335
00:25:12.310 --> 00:25:13.800
It's super-exclusive.

336
00:25:13.879 --> 00:25:17.337
I mean, I don't know... I don't know if I can get a ticket, you know.

337
00:25:17.415 --> 00:25:20.241
I mean, I'll be off, like, a day or two, max.

338
00:25:20.242 --> 00:25:22.115
If you want, I'll try to get two tickets,

339
00:25:22.187 --> 00:25:24.451
but I'm not sure I can get two tickets. Yeah.

340
00:25:25.123 --> 00:25:27.353
To a wine auction? In Livorno?

341
00:25:27.692 --> 00:25:29.819
Yeah, it sounds terrible. I know.

342
00:25:29.895 --> 00:25:31.289
I'm sorry. I'm so...

343
00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:34.230
I feel terrible now. I feel terrible. I mean, it's...

344
00:25:35.033 --> 00:25:36.898
I miss you already, you know.

345
00:25:39.938 --> 00:25:42.839
I'll be back very soon. Okay? Yeah. Be careful.

346
00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:49.872
Bye! Ciao.

347
00:26:18.577 --> 00:26:20.841
Listen to what this poor girl writes.

348
00:26:20.912 --> 00:26:24.245
"Juliet, is there anything worse in this world than a man?"

349
00:26:28.887 --> 00:26:30.218
Excuse me.

350
00:26:31.456 --> 00:26:36.359
I'm sorry to interrupt, but are you the Secretaries of Juliet?

351
00:26:36.761 --> 00:26:38.820
Yes, that's us. Great.

352
00:26:38.897 --> 00:26:40.501
May I ask, which one of you

353
00:26:40.502 --> 00:26:43.426
wrote this letter to my grandmother, Claire Smith?

354
00:26:44.669 --> 00:26:47.695
I wrote that. And I can't believe that it found you.

355
00:26:47.772 --> 00:26:50.866
Yes. We Brits tend to stay in our family homes.

356
00:26:51.276 --> 00:26:55.110
Yeah. This is amazing. It's less than a week since I mailed it.

357
00:26:56.147 --> 00:26:58.775
It was a very thoughtful letter.

358
00:26:59.217 --> 00:27:02.152
Now, what were you thinking?

359
00:27:04.222 --> 00:27:07.453
I was thinking that she deserved an answer.

360
00:27:07.525 --> 00:27:09.618
Yeah, 50 years ago, maybe. Not now.

361
00:27:09.694 --> 00:27:12.822
I'm sorry. I didn't know true love had an expiration date.

362
00:27:13.465 --> 00:27:15.831
True love? Are you joking?

363
00:27:16.201 --> 00:27:18.692
Could you imagine what would've happened if she hadn't seen sense?

364
00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:20.965
Well, yeah, you wouldn't be here, and that would be an upside.

365
00:27:21.039 --> 00:27:22.116
What are you doing here anyway?

366
00:27:22.117 --> 00:27:24.576
What, are you some lonely American girl

367
00:27:24.577 --> 00:27:26.044
who has to live vicariously through others?

368
00:27:26.111 --> 00:27:28.045
I'm not lonely. I am engaged.

369
00:27:29.347 --> 00:27:31.474
My condolences to the groom.

370
00:27:41.793 --> 00:27:43.158
Excuse me!

371
00:27:43.728 --> 00:27:44.578
So that's it?

372
00:27:44.579 --> 00:27:46.596
You've come all the way from London to give me a lecture?

373
00:27:46.665 --> 00:27:49.498
No, I came because I couldn't let my grandmother come on her own.

374
00:27:49.567 --> 00:27:52.058
Claire is here? Why?

375
00:27:52.137 --> 00:27:54.833
I think even you might be able to work that one out.

376
00:27:56.775 --> 00:27:58.106
Oh, my God!

377
00:27:58.576 --> 00:28:01.636
That's awesome! She's come to find her Lorenzo!

378
00:28:02.947 --> 00:28:04.175
Awesome?

379
00:28:07.318 --> 00:28:10.879
And if Lorenzo doesn't want to see her, has forgotten her,

380
00:28:10.955 --> 00:28:15.756
is gravely ill or dead, let's say, just for fun, then what?

381
00:28:18.463 --> 00:28:19.828
Thought so.

382
00:28:20.398 --> 00:28:22.593
I want to meet her. I'd like to meet Claire.

383
00:28:22.667 --> 00:28:25.101
And I want to play for Manchester United, but I'm shit at football.

384
00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:26.637
You don't think that she wants to meet me?

385
00:28:26.705 --> 00:28:29.824
I honestly think she has no interest meeting a woman

386
00:28:29.825 --> 00:28:33.050
that can manage to jam, "Oh, my God" and "awesome"

387
00:28:33.051 --> 00:28:35.106
into the same sentence.

388
00:29:08.246 --> 00:29:11.477
Gran, they're closing up. We should go now.

389
00:29:27.232 --> 00:29:31.259
Excuse me. Claire? Hi. Hi. My name is Sophie,

390
00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:34.102
and I wrote you the letter.

391
00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:37.571
Letter? From Juliet?

392
00:29:38.910 --> 00:29:40.707
Goodness! Thank you.

393
00:29:42.547 --> 00:29:44.674
Thank you. But however did you find us?

394
00:29:44.749 --> 00:29:47.115
Well, your grandson found me, actually. You did?

395
00:29:47.185 --> 00:29:48.675
Right, well, the thing is,

396
00:29:48.753 --> 00:29:50.880
I thought we should... Yeah, well, he's very sweet

397
00:29:50.955 --> 00:29:56.484
and very charming, and he thought that you might like to meet me.

398
00:29:57.495 --> 00:29:59.520
Uncharacteristically thoughtful of you, dear.

399
00:29:59.597 --> 00:30:01.792
Yes, well, under the circumstances,

400
00:30:01.866 --> 00:30:05.063
it seems a little churlish not to meet the woman responsible

401
00:30:05.136 --> 00:30:07.297
for this voyage of lunacy. Okay.

402
00:30:07.372 --> 00:30:10.500
Charlie doesn't approve, which makes this all the more fun.

403
00:30:12.777 --> 00:30:15.473
We were just going for a glass of wine. Would you like to join us?

404
00:30:15.547 --> 00:30:16.639
Absolutely not.

405
00:30:16.714 --> 00:30:17.738
What? Absolutely not.

406
00:30:17.816 --> 00:30:20.614
It's been a long day. It's too long a day.

407
00:30:20.685 --> 00:30:23.119
Okay, darling. Have a good sleep. But I...

408
00:30:23.188 --> 00:30:25.622
Do you know somewhere we could go?

409
00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:28.352
I actually have the perfect place.

410
00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:34.825
I can't believe you're really here.

411
00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:37.802
I was taking an art course in Tuscany,

412
00:30:37.803 --> 00:30:39.794
staying with a family outside Siena,

413
00:30:40.338 --> 00:30:45.776
and they had a son Lorenzo, and it was love at first sight.

414
00:30:46.911 --> 00:30:49.072
He had such gentle blue eyes.

415
00:30:49.447 --> 00:30:51.972
And he told me I was very beautiful.

416
00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:05.185
And he gave me this ring.

417
00:31:07.298 --> 00:31:08.856
We wanted to get married,

418
00:31:08.933 --> 00:31:11.568
but I'd got exams back in England

419
00:31:11.569 --> 00:31:13.393
and my parents would never have agreed,

420
00:31:13.394 --> 00:31:15.138
and I got scared,

421
00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:17.630
and I ran away.

422
00:31:19.944 --> 00:31:21.036
Now

423
00:31:24.048 --> 00:31:26.642
what I really want is to

424
00:31:27.986 --> 00:31:32.150
tell him that I'm so sorry I was such a coward.

425
00:31:32.857 --> 00:31:35.917
Of course, a card or letter is just too simple.

426
00:31:36.527 --> 00:31:39.018
So, you've come to find your Lorenzo?

427
00:31:39.564 --> 00:31:42.055
Yes, and I think I know where he is.

428
00:31:42.433 --> 00:31:43.661
Where?

429
00:31:43.735 --> 00:31:45.965
On the farm, just near Siena.

430
00:31:47.505 --> 00:31:50.030
Charlie's driving me down tomorrow.

431
00:31:50.108 --> 00:31:52.736
Only because I didn't want you to hitchhike.

432
00:31:54.012 --> 00:31:56.378
Well, he thinks it's a big mistake.

433
00:31:56.447 --> 00:31:59.007
Because you don't have a romantic bone in your body.

434
00:31:59.083 --> 00:32:01.881
No, no. It's because I am a realist.

435
00:32:01.953 --> 00:32:04.319
No. Because you are an Englishman!

436
00:32:04.389 --> 00:32:05.856
Cold as fish!

437
00:32:06.291 --> 00:32:09.749
Right. And who was it that wrote Romeo and Juliet?

438
00:32:09.827 --> 00:32:12.625
Williamo Shakespearelli, a great Italian.

439
00:32:14.365 --> 00:32:16.060
They're mad. They're mad.

440
00:32:17.235 --> 00:32:19.032
Sophie, my dear.

441
00:32:20.171 --> 00:32:22.639
Good night. Thank you so much.

442
00:32:23.441 --> 00:32:24.669
Thank you.

443
00:32:28.012 --> 00:32:30.207
Good night. Good night, everybody.

444
00:32:30.715 --> 00:32:33.047
Good night. Charlie, let's go.

445
00:32:33.117 --> 00:32:34.948
Good night, ladies. Ciao. Good luck.

446
00:32:35.019 --> 00:32:36.043
Bye. Ciao.

447
00:32:36.621 --> 00:32:38.987
How wonderful would it be if she found her Romeo?

448
00:32:42.193 --> 00:32:46.425
Imagine if she did, 50 years later, all because you found that letter.

449
00:32:46.497 --> 00:32:50.263
Nobody would believe it if it weren't true, you know.

450
00:32:59.177 --> 00:33:01.202
Wait! Wait!

451
00:33:01.279 --> 00:33:03.770
Oh, for... Here she comes. Sorry.

452
00:33:05.016 --> 00:33:07.883
May I come with you to find Lorenzo? Come with us?

453
00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:10.117
What about this fianc?of yours?

454
00:33:10.188 --> 00:33:12.520
You're in the city of love and you want to come with us?

455
00:33:12.590 --> 00:33:16.219
Yes, I do. Because he's busy and I'm free. And I mean...

456
00:33:16.294 --> 00:33:17.508
Of course, if it's not an intrusion.

457
00:33:17.509 --> 00:33:18.991
No, no, it's not an intrusion.

458
00:33:19.063 --> 00:33:20.223
I just... Listen,

459
00:33:20.298 --> 00:33:22.198
I do have one thing to tell you first. It's not an intrusion.

460
00:33:22.266 --> 00:33:23.927
This is not entirely selfless.

461
00:33:24.002 --> 00:33:26.800
It started out as a simple reply to your letter,

462
00:33:26.871 --> 00:33:30.238
and now I think it's so amazing what you're doing,

463
00:33:30.842 --> 00:33:33.936
and I'd love to write about it if it's okay. A journalist.

464
00:33:34.012 --> 00:33:36.981
Gran, a journalist trying to pry into our private lives.

465
00:33:37.048 --> 00:33:39.846
Charlie, come on. It's hardly state secrets, is it?

466
00:33:39.917 --> 00:33:42.784
I'm not a journalist. I would like to be some day, but...

467
00:33:42.854 --> 00:33:45.118
And you think Gran's story is your ticket?

468
00:33:45.189 --> 00:33:46.952
I think it's a story worth telling.

469
00:33:47.025 --> 00:33:48.549
I'd love to have you with us.

470
00:33:48.626 --> 00:33:51.117
Really? Yes. Of course.

471
00:33:51.529 --> 00:33:52.621
We would.

472
00:34:39.777 --> 00:34:42.678
How much time did you actually spend with Lorenzo?

473
00:34:43.114 --> 00:34:45.014
Every second that we could.

474
00:34:45.516 --> 00:34:49.850
I'd go to meet him after class, to the farm where he was working,

475
00:34:49.921 --> 00:34:51.823
and we'd sit under the trees

476
00:34:51.824 --> 00:34:55.300
and we'd share some bread with tomatoes and olive oil.

477
00:34:55.335 --> 00:34:56.651
It's so romantic. Yeah.

478
00:34:56.727 --> 00:34:58.456
What's so romantic about eating in the dirt?

479
00:34:58.529 --> 00:34:59.518
Really?

480
00:34:59.597 --> 00:35:02.566
The dirt. Well, you see, he loved the earth.

481
00:35:03.134 --> 00:35:07.070
He loved talking to me about the rain

482
00:35:08.406 --> 00:35:10.704
and the springs of fresh water,

483
00:35:11.175 --> 00:35:13.803
and we'd walk for miles and miles.

484
00:35:14.145 --> 00:35:16.272
We'd lie in the dry grass.

485
00:35:16.814 --> 00:35:18.611
I can still remember

486
00:35:19.884 --> 00:35:23.718
the smell of his sweet, hot sweat and...

487
00:35:24.322 --> 00:35:26.984
Okay. Okay, Gran. We get the picture.

488
00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:32.327
So you must feel like you're about to find your long-lost soul mate.

489
00:35:32.396 --> 00:35:33.522
Yes.

490
00:35:33.598 --> 00:35:35.531
Her soul mate was my grandfather.

491
00:35:35.532 --> 00:35:36.932
Let's not invalidate my entire existence.

492
00:35:37.001 --> 00:35:40.835
Okay, I'm sorry, I just... That's clearly not what I meant.

493
00:35:41.639 --> 00:35:43.937
Charlie's just like my husband.

494
00:35:45.042 --> 00:35:46.441
Both skeptics.

495
00:35:46.511 --> 00:35:50.447
My husband always insisted that love was nothing but hormones.

496
00:35:50.515 --> 00:35:54.315
But you see, underneath, he had such a warm, passionate heart.

497
00:35:54.852 --> 00:35:56.979
And Charlie's just the same.

498
00:36:01.159 --> 00:36:03.992
Tell me about the guy you're in love with.

499
00:36:06.297 --> 00:36:07.491
Victor.

500
00:36:08.399 --> 00:36:09.491
Yeah.

501
00:36:16.174 --> 00:36:20.372
Well, Victor is a chef, an amazing chef. Wow.

502
00:36:20.444 --> 00:36:24.175
Yeah. He's opening his own restaurant in New York.

503
00:36:25.049 --> 00:36:29.679
And that's the reason we're here. We're meeting suppliers all around.

504
00:36:29.754 --> 00:36:34.714
And he's actually... He's in Livorno at a wine auction as we speak.

505
00:36:36.527 --> 00:36:37.858
That sounds fun.

506
00:36:38.729 --> 00:36:40.720
For him. Yeah.

507
00:36:44.936 --> 00:36:47.461
Well, and do your parents like him?

508
00:36:49.941 --> 00:36:53.399
Yeah, my dad. My dad likes him.

509
00:36:54.979 --> 00:36:57.743
My mom left us when I was nine.

510
00:36:59.217 --> 00:37:00.445
Oh, dear.

511
00:37:01.819 --> 00:37:03.640
Oh, dear. I'm sorry about that.

512
00:37:03.641 --> 00:37:06.052
That's okay. It was a really long time ago.

513
00:37:18.169 --> 00:37:19.727
Slowly, slowly.

514
00:37:45.062 --> 00:37:47.189
How do you want to go about this?

515
00:37:49.267 --> 00:37:50.962
Ring the doorbell.

516
00:38:02.046 --> 00:38:05.675
What if the wife answers and goes into a fit of jealous rage? Gran?

517
00:38:08.486 --> 00:38:10.386
Well, I'd be flattered.

518
00:38:16.227 --> 00:38:19.390
I'm more worried that he won't remember me.

519
00:38:20.298 --> 00:38:22.052
Right. Let's go home then, Gran,

520
00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:23.735
before this all ends in tears.

521
00:38:30.808 --> 00:38:32.639
Come on. There's no one in.

522
00:38:33.010 --> 00:38:34.375
I'm gonna check the back.

523
00:38:34.445 --> 00:38:35.912
There's no... We made our best effort.

524
00:38:35.980 --> 00:38:38.414
There's no need to prolong the disappointment.

525
00:38:52.963 --> 00:38:54.123
It's me, yeah.

526
00:38:54.665 --> 00:38:57.361
Good afternoon. I'm Charlie Wyman.

527
00:38:57.435 --> 00:38:59.926
This is my grandmother, Claire,

528
00:39:00.004 --> 00:39:01.335
and Sophie. Ciao.

529
00:39:03.341 --> 00:39:05.707
I'm Claire. Do you remember?

530
00:39:07.311 --> 00:39:08.505
Claire.

531
00:39:13.017 --> 00:39:14.507
It's not him.

532
00:39:15.953 --> 00:39:17.443
Are you sure?

533
00:39:17.922 --> 00:39:19.617
It's not his eyes.

534
00:39:25.463 --> 00:39:27.124
Let's make sure.

535
00:39:27.198 --> 00:39:30.827
Do you remember what you were doing in the summer of 1957?

536
00:39:32.169 --> 00:39:35.969
The summer of 1957, I will never forget,

537
00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:40.568
I met the most beautiful girl with long, blonde hair.

538
00:39:40.644 --> 00:39:41.736
Nana!

539
00:39:41.812 --> 00:39:43.609
We married that year.

540
00:39:46.751 --> 00:39:48.844
I curse that woman! What?

541
00:39:48.919 --> 00:39:49.977
Oh, no! Right.

542
00:39:50.054 --> 00:39:52.501
I curse her eyes! I curse her teeth!

543
00:39:52.502 --> 00:39:53.684
I curse her mustache!

544
00:39:54.392 --> 00:39:59.159
But why are you asking? Why you bring up such a horrible memory?

545
00:40:00.398 --> 00:40:01.956
Well, that went well.

546
00:40:02.032 --> 00:40:04.432
Can't say we didn't try, Nana. I'm sorry. Truly.

547
00:40:04.502 --> 00:40:05.628
Are you for real?

548
00:40:05.703 --> 00:40:07.501
If I actually knew what that question meant,

549
00:40:07.502 --> 00:40:09.333
I might attempt to answer it.

550
00:40:09.407 --> 00:40:12.308
He can't be the only Lorenzo Bartolini in Tuscany.

551
00:40:14.412 --> 00:40:16.141
Well, he's the only one around here, so...

552
00:40:16.213 --> 00:40:19.580
I haven't flown all the way here to pack it in now.

553
00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:21.015
Nana, what do you want to do?

554
00:40:21.085 --> 00:40:23.055
You want to start knocking on every door

555
00:40:23.056 --> 00:40:25.284
asking if Lorenzo's in? I mean...

556
00:40:27.091 --> 00:40:28.558
How many can there be?

557
00:40:29.794 --> 00:40:31.261
Seventy-four?

558
00:40:31.529 --> 00:40:35.556
Seventy-four Lorenzo Bartolinis and you want to go on a scavenger hunt?

559
00:40:35.633 --> 00:40:37.157
Impossible. We're going home in the morning.

560
00:40:37.234 --> 00:40:40.761
Come on, Charlie, will you calm down? Sophie, what do you think?

561
00:40:40.838 --> 00:40:43.932
Gran, what do you mean, what does she think? She doesn't think.

562
00:40:44.008 --> 00:40:48.001
This was her brilliant idea. It's over! Done! We found Nemo.

563
00:40:48.078 --> 00:40:49.636
The curtains have closed.

564
00:40:52.116 --> 00:40:54.448
Sophie. Listen, this is my job.

565
00:40:54.752 --> 00:40:58.381
I find things. And through process of elimination

566
00:40:58.789 --> 00:41:02.225
and caveat emptor, which will require assembling a few more facts,

567
00:41:02.293 --> 00:41:04.454
we can narrow this down considerably.

568
00:41:04.528 --> 00:41:07.554
Did Ms. Awesome For Real just use caveat emptor?

569
00:41:07.631 --> 00:41:11.727
Oh, please, all right. I went to Brown. Double Major. Minor in Latin.

570
00:41:11.802 --> 00:41:13.736
This is madness. It's madness.

571
00:41:14.305 --> 00:41:18.002
Charlie, didn't you say you wanted to go and swim?

572
00:41:18.075 --> 00:41:21.272
We are working. Go away. This is utter, complete mad...

573
00:41:21.345 --> 00:41:22.471
Work!

574
00:41:23.948 --> 00:41:25.472
I can't believe it.

575
00:41:26.383 --> 00:41:27.407
Ridiculous.

576
00:41:32.857 --> 00:41:34.449
He's a good-looking sort.

577
00:41:35.559 --> 00:41:36.787
Isn't he?

578
00:41:41.899 --> 00:41:46.393
All right. Are you sure that Lorenzo would never have left this area?

579
00:41:46.904 --> 00:41:50.738
He loved this land. I'm quite certain.

580
00:41:54.378 --> 00:41:55.538
May I?

581
00:42:09.793 --> 00:42:11.260
Our Lorenzos.

582
00:42:18.536 --> 00:42:21.835
Are you sure that you have time for all this?

583
00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:34.412
Ciao. How are you?

584
00:42:35.052 --> 00:42:37.043
I'm good. How are you?

585
00:42:37.121 --> 00:42:39.487
You know what? I was just witnessing this most incredible,

586
00:42:39.557 --> 00:42:40.990
incredible Sauterne auction.

587
00:42:41.058 --> 00:42:43.424
It was just beautiful. It was incredible. How's it going?

588
00:42:43.561 --> 00:42:45.961
I'm good. I'm in Siena right now.

589
00:42:46.030 --> 00:42:47.497
What? In Siena?

590
00:42:47.565 --> 00:42:50.056
What are you doing in Siena? I'm writing a story.

591
00:42:50.134 --> 00:42:52.295
We're kind of on a bit of an adventure,

592
00:42:52.369 --> 00:42:55.702
looking for somebody that she used to know 50 years ago.

593
00:42:55.773 --> 00:42:56.967
You know what, baby? You know what?

594
00:42:57.041 --> 00:42:59.669
I mean, this isn't a good time because I'm in the middle of something,

595
00:42:59.743 --> 00:43:01.456
but, I mean, I'm glad you called

596
00:43:01.457 --> 00:43:03.253
because I was actually just gonna call you right now.

597
00:43:03.581 --> 00:43:04.975
You know, the opportunities here

598
00:43:04.976 --> 00:43:07.143
are really, really, really incredible, you know,

599
00:43:07.217 --> 00:43:09.617
and I think I'm gonna have to stay till Friday.

600
00:43:09.954 --> 00:43:11.219
Can I stay or should I skip it?

601
00:43:11.220 --> 00:43:12.287
I mean, I feel horrible, but...

602
00:43:12.356 --> 00:43:15.450
No, do it. Please. I'm gonna need until Friday, as well.

603
00:43:15.759 --> 00:43:19.195
Great. So Friday, all right? Friday. Friday's good. Okay. Win-win.

604
00:43:25.836 --> 00:43:27.098
Claire?

605
00:43:28.238 --> 00:43:29.227
I'm free.

606
00:43:30.474 --> 00:43:31.964
Free for what? Free for what? She's coming.

607
00:43:32.042 --> 00:43:33.942
She's coming with us. She's coming? Splendid.

608
00:43:34.011 --> 00:43:35.945
That's splendid! Fantastic!

609
00:44:02.011 --> 00:44:05.945
Too bad I'm not the Lorenzo you're looking for.

610
00:44:06.011 --> 00:44:09.945
If you don't find him, come back.

611
00:44:11.011 --> 00:44:12.945
I'll make you a fish dinner.

612
00:44:14.218 --> 00:44:15.776
Okay? Okay.

613
00:44:23.994 --> 00:44:25.359
Good luck.

614
00:44:57.327 --> 00:44:58.817
It's not him.

615
00:44:59.163 --> 00:45:00.687
Oh, my God. No!

616
00:45:30.794 --> 00:45:33.160
Well, wouldn't this be nice, Gran?

617
00:45:33.230 --> 00:45:36.666
Going from a boy who works the fields to a man who owns them.

618
00:45:36.734 --> 00:45:39.225
And you got to skip the messy bits.

619
00:45:42.039 --> 00:45:44.064
Life is the messy bits.

620
00:45:51.715 --> 00:45:53.410
Peccato it wasn't me.

621
00:45:54.985 --> 00:45:56.077
Yes.

622
00:45:58.589 --> 00:46:00.819
I would have never let you go.

623
00:46:02.359 --> 00:46:03.724
Believe me.

624
00:46:14.738 --> 00:46:18.003
What is it with you and Italian men? They fall at your feet.

625
00:46:18.809 --> 00:46:21.437
It's such a shame. I think I could've been happy here.

626
00:46:21.512 --> 00:46:24.106
Happy? Did your grandson just say happy?

627
00:46:24.515 --> 00:46:25.948
I said could have been.

628
00:46:26.049 --> 00:46:28.381
Let's not get carried away. I have a reputation to uphold.

629
00:46:31.455 --> 00:46:36.256
Charlie, why don't you tell Sophi about your pro bono legal work?

630
00:46:36.326 --> 00:46:38.658
I can't imagine she'd have much interest, Gran.

631
00:46:38.729 --> 00:46:41.220
Try me. You know the sort of thing,

632
00:46:41.698 --> 00:46:43.876
defending the defenseless, preserving human rights,

633
00:46:43.877 --> 00:46:45.931
helping refugees get asylum.

634
00:46:46.503 --> 00:46:48.664
You look surprised. I'm so surprised.

635
00:46:48.739 --> 00:46:52.573
I just didn't have you pinned down as the save-humanity type at all.

636
00:46:54.011 --> 00:46:55.376
Really?

637
00:46:57.314 --> 00:47:00.772
And what type did you have me pinned down for exactly?

638
00:47:00.851 --> 00:47:04.048
Well, I was leaning towards elitist Oxford prig,

639
00:47:04.121 --> 00:47:05.138
but now that you mention it,

640
00:47:05.139 --> 00:47:07.167
self-satisfied do-gooder works, as well.

641
00:47:07.958 --> 00:47:10.085
I guess you're a poor judge of character then.

642
00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:12.788
But she does deal with facts, Charlie.

643
00:47:15.065 --> 00:47:16.157
Okay.

644
00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:18.734
I'm off to bed. Okay.

645
00:47:19.903 --> 00:47:21.894
Good night, darling. Good night. Sleep well.

646
00:47:21.972 --> 00:47:24.532
May I walk you to your room? Be nice to each other.

647
00:47:30.047 --> 00:47:31.537
She is awesome.

648
00:47:32.583 --> 00:47:34.642
I respect your usage of the word this one instance.

649
00:47:34.718 --> 00:47:37.016
Oh, I'm flattered. Thank you for approving.

650
00:47:37.087 --> 00:47:38.850
You're very welcome.

651
00:47:42.192 --> 00:47:44.752
Right. Well. Should we hit the sack?

652
00:47:45.395 --> 00:47:46.919
Sorry. I guess I...

653
00:47:49.032 --> 00:47:51.822
Well, as much as I'd love to sit here

654
00:47:51.823 --> 00:47:54.403
and drink the whole bottle of Caparzo...

655
00:47:54.805 --> 00:47:57.831
No, go to bed. You should. Good night, Charlie.

656
00:47:59.209 --> 00:48:00.403
Right.

657
00:48:00.777 --> 00:48:02.904
Well, good night. Good night.

658
00:48:12.689 --> 00:48:15.180
Forgive me. Where are my manners?

659
00:48:15.259 --> 00:48:18.023
You know, I've been wondering that since I met you.

660
00:48:18.095 --> 00:48:20.461
Yes, well, I don't know what it is,

661
00:48:21.164 --> 00:48:23.564
but you seem to bring out the very worst in me.

662
00:48:24.134 --> 00:48:26.602
Somehow it's my fault. All of this is your fault.

663
00:48:26.670 --> 00:48:28.900
You wrote that blasted letter.

664
00:48:30.908 --> 00:48:33.741
Nonetheless, may I walk you to your room?

665
00:48:34.544 --> 00:48:37.206
You know, I'm not the buttoned-up,

666
00:48:37.281 --> 00:48:39.806
buttock-clenching killjoy you try to make me out as.

667
00:48:39.883 --> 00:48:43.944
Right. And I made you argue that true love is bollocks.

668
00:48:45.389 --> 00:48:46.583
Guilty.

669
00:48:49.593 --> 00:48:52.619
It's just that I'm genuinely worried for her.

670
00:48:53.430 --> 00:48:55.762
I know she may seem carefree,

671
00:48:57.434 --> 00:49:00.232
but her life hasn't been all that simple.

672
00:49:02.272 --> 00:49:04.502
Is it that unreasonable for me to worry?

673
00:49:04.574 --> 00:49:08.510
No, but I have a really good feeling about this. You'll see.

674
00:49:08.979 --> 00:49:10.913
Let's hope you're right.

675
00:49:34.004 --> 00:49:35.471
Signora Claire.

676
00:49:38.575 --> 00:49:40.202
Signor Bartolini.

677
00:49:40.600 --> 00:49:44.400
Do you remember me? I'm Claire Smith.

678
00:49:46.500 --> 00:49:52.300
Your beautiful face, it's so familiar...

679
00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:00.477
Good evening.

680
00:50:45.642 --> 00:50:47.371
We're not done yet.

681
00:50:49.046 --> 00:50:51.037
Of course we're not, darling.

682
00:51:21.178 --> 00:51:22.270
Hello.

683
00:51:22.612 --> 00:51:24.204
Hello? Hello.

684
00:51:27.484 --> 00:51:29.884
Hi. Is Claire with you? No?

685
00:51:30.287 --> 00:51:32.585
No, no. She's turned in early, actually.

686
00:51:32.656 --> 00:51:33.884
Okay.

687
00:51:33.957 --> 00:51:35.857
I was gonna ask if you wanted dinner, but...

688
00:51:35.926 --> 00:51:38.520
I just... I wanted to get started on my story.

689
00:51:39.062 --> 00:51:40.359
How's it coming?

690
00:51:40.430 --> 00:51:42.330
It's coming okay.

691
00:51:43.366 --> 00:51:44.765
Am I in it?

692
00:51:44.835 --> 00:51:47.201
You may be. Why? Can I read just a little bit?

693
00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:49.534
Are you kidding me? Come on, just a little bit.

694
00:51:49.606 --> 00:51:51.130
Absolutely not.

695
00:51:52.109 --> 00:51:54.771
How am I to be sure that I'm being properly portrayed?

696
00:51:54.845 --> 00:51:57.143
No, you are. Believe me. You are.

697
00:51:57.214 --> 00:52:00.183
What's that supposed to mean? It means good night, Charlie.

698
00:52:02.886 --> 00:52:04.820
Good night. Okay.

699
00:52:05.989 --> 00:52:07.081
Yeah.

700
00:52:10.527 --> 00:52:11.858
Good night.

701
00:52:15.866 --> 00:52:17.561
What? Okay. Is this

702
00:52:19.169 --> 00:52:22.036
one of those situations where you're really saying the opposite

703
00:52:22.105 --> 00:52:24.164
of what you actually mean?

704
00:52:24.875 --> 00:52:25.899
No.

705
00:52:26.276 --> 00:52:27.436
Right.

706
00:52:28.211 --> 00:52:29.576
Good night then.

707
00:52:29.646 --> 00:52:31.341
Good night, Charlie.

708
00:52:48.398 --> 00:52:49.865
Good morning.

709
00:52:50.133 --> 00:52:51.566
Good morning.

710
00:52:51.635 --> 00:52:53.364
Gran wants to sleep in this morning.

711
00:52:53.436 --> 00:52:55.904
Is she okay? I mean, does she need anything?

712
00:52:55.972 --> 00:52:59.100
No need. She's tough as old boots, that one.

713
00:52:59.442 --> 00:53:01.239
Churchill in a dress.

714
00:53:03.613 --> 00:53:06.878
So, I thought I'd see a bit of Siena since we're here.

715
00:53:06.950 --> 00:53:08.577
It's a good idea.

716
00:53:09.753 --> 00:53:12.415
I suppose you want to keep on writing.

717
00:53:13.723 --> 00:53:14.951
I mean...

718
00:53:15.926 --> 00:53:17.553
Work, work, work.

719
00:53:18.028 --> 00:53:19.586
Admirable trait.

720
00:53:22.966 --> 00:53:25.332
Right, of course. Carry on then.

721
00:53:28.038 --> 00:53:29.300
Charlie?

722
00:53:32.209 --> 00:53:33.972
Since we're here...

723
00:53:36.346 --> 00:53:40.680
I hate to compliment you, but what you're doing for Claire is really...

724
00:53:41.151 --> 00:53:42.948
It's sweet, and I...

725
00:53:43.620 --> 00:53:47.351
I imagine that you'd rather be spending your holiday elsewhere,

726
00:53:47.991 --> 00:53:49.893
so did you volunteer your services, or did your parents?

727
00:53:49.894 --> 00:53:51.096
I mean, where do they fit in?

728
00:53:54.564 --> 00:53:57.624
I'd like to think they're someplace nice.

729
00:54:00.770 --> 00:54:03.898
They were killed in a car accident when I was 10.

730
00:54:07.577 --> 00:54:08.908
I'm sorry.

731
00:54:10.780 --> 00:54:12.270
God, I'm sorry.

732
00:54:13.783 --> 00:54:16.274
Thank you, Sophie. I appreciate it.

733
00:54:17.354 --> 00:54:19.288
It was a difficult time.

734
00:54:20.657 --> 00:54:22.318
Gran lost her son.

735
00:54:23.126 --> 00:54:24.821
I lost my parents.

736
00:54:26.529 --> 00:54:29.828
I'm not a big believer in happy endings, I'm afraid.

737
00:54:31.067 --> 00:54:32.636
Anyway, Gran stepped in.

738
00:54:32.800 --> 00:54:36.096
Took an angry, obnoxious, young boy under her wing

739
00:54:36.640 --> 00:54:39.837
and made me the simply unpleasant man I am today.

740
00:54:43.179 --> 00:54:46.114
So, isn't your fiance starting to get just a tad lonely?

741
00:54:46.182 --> 00:54:49.345
You know, Victor's having the time of his life right now.

742
00:54:49.419 --> 00:54:52.149
And I doubt he even notices that I'm gone.

743
00:54:52.222 --> 00:54:53.780
What about you?

744
00:54:54.190 --> 00:54:58.593
Don't you have a girlfriend? That is a very, very long story.

745
00:54:58.928 --> 00:55:00.418
Yeah? I'm not surprised.

746
00:55:00.497 --> 00:55:02.931
It's been about a year since I've seen Patricia.

747
00:55:02.999 --> 00:55:04.990
And you broke up with her?

748
00:55:07.070 --> 00:55:09.480
Are you insinuating that I'm more the type to be dumped

749
00:55:09.481 --> 00:55:11.405
rather than do the dumping?

750
00:55:11.474 --> 00:55:12.498
Maybe I am.

751
00:55:12.575 --> 00:55:15.510
Actually, I did break up with Patricia. This time.

752
00:55:24.921 --> 00:55:26.650
Okay, that's enough.

753
00:55:31.828 --> 00:55:33.625
All right. That's enough.

754
00:55:35.532 --> 00:55:37.523
Sophie... That's enough.

755
00:55:38.101 --> 00:55:40.934
Your writing is really good. Thank you.

756
00:55:41.004 --> 00:55:43.632
No. It's really, really, really good.

757
00:55:44.974 --> 00:55:46.999
Why are you so surprised?

758
00:55:47.577 --> 00:55:49.670
How come you've never shown your work to anyone?

759
00:55:49.746 --> 00:55:52.010
I never feel like it's finished. Why?

760
00:55:52.082 --> 00:55:54.607
Because I guess I'm a perfectionist.

761
00:55:55.151 --> 00:55:58.314
You know that's just another way of saying "I'm a chicken"?

762
00:55:58.388 --> 00:56:01.323
Listen. You've got nothing to be afraid of.

763
00:56:01.758 --> 00:56:04.454
You're not a fact checker. You're a writer.

764
00:56:06.930 --> 00:56:09.455
What was that for? I'm not a chicken.

765
00:56:11.568 --> 00:56:13.536
And I'm not a gentleman.

766
00:56:14.938 --> 00:56:16.997
Do you think we should get back to Claire?

767
00:56:17.073 --> 00:56:19.200
Yeah. Many more Lorenzos.

768
00:56:21.010 --> 00:56:22.136
Okay.

769
00:56:26.316 --> 00:56:28.375
We should get back to Claire.

770
00:56:30.720 --> 00:56:33.052
I am Lorenzo Bartolini!

771
00:56:38.695 --> 00:56:41.220
Take him! Take him!

772
00:56:41.531 --> 00:56:43.556
I am Lorenzo Bartolini!

773
00:56:44.100 --> 00:56:46.159
I am Lorenzo Bartolini!

774
00:56:49.038 --> 00:56:50.835
I am Lorenzo Bartolini!

775
00:56:57.180 --> 00:56:58.841
Do you know what you are?

776
00:56:58.915 --> 00:57:01.042
You are the Montagues and the Capulets.

777
00:57:01.117 --> 00:57:06.282
You mean... Well, at least I'm not Romeo.

778
00:57:06.556 --> 00:57:08.622
That's the understatement of the century.

779
00:57:08.623 --> 00:57:10.288
No, no, no, because you see,

780
00:57:11.261 --> 00:57:12.775
if I found the love of my life,

781
00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:14.720
I wouldn't stand there like an idiot

782
00:57:14.798 --> 00:57:16.789
whispering in a garden.

783
00:57:17.367 --> 00:57:21.565
I would just grab her from that blasted balcony and be done with it.

784
00:57:25.508 --> 00:57:27.533
Do you believe in destiny?

785
00:57:28.711 --> 00:57:29.871
Do you?

786
00:57:30.713 --> 00:57:32.305
Yeah, I think so.

787
00:57:32.649 --> 00:57:35.709
I think it's destiny that I'm here, right now.

788
00:57:41.124 --> 00:57:42.523
With Claire.

789
00:57:43.760 --> 00:57:46.126
Of course. Of course. With Claire.

790
00:58:19.629 --> 00:58:21.620
Well, this is the street.

791
00:58:25.502 --> 00:58:28.096
Well, there's 2. Here's number 2.

792
00:59:45.882 --> 00:59:47.315
This is what I feared the most.

793
00:59:47.383 --> 00:59:48.804
Charlie, we don't even know if it's him.

794
00:59:48.805 --> 00:59:50.369
I hope it's not. I hope it's not.

795
00:59:51.788 --> 00:59:54.814
Either way, we're finished. We're done. No. Not here.

796
00:59:55.458 --> 00:59:57.050
When do we stop?

797
00:59:57.794 --> 01:00:00.262
Over the next hill? Or the next? Scour the whole country?

798
01:00:00.330 --> 01:00:01.991
No. No. It ends here.

799
01:00:02.065 --> 01:00:05.159
There are other Lorenzos not too far away from here.

800
01:00:05.234 --> 01:00:07.794
It's not the ending you want, is it?

801
01:00:08.438 --> 01:00:09.962
This isn't just a clever story

802
01:00:10.039 --> 01:00:11.506
with a clever ending. Stop it, Charlie.

803
01:00:11.574 --> 01:00:13.488
It's our life. It's my Gran's life.

804
01:00:13.489 --> 01:00:14.910
Charlie, it's not about a story.

805
01:00:14.978 --> 01:00:17.469
No, no, no, she's already lost the man once.

806
01:00:19.048 --> 01:00:21.676
How many more times would you have her lose him?

807
01:00:22.385 --> 01:00:24.478
You know what? I don't expect you to understand any of this

808
01:00:24.554 --> 01:00:27.580
because you don't know a thing about real loss.

809
01:00:28.825 --> 01:00:29.951
Yeah.

810
01:00:43.573 --> 01:00:45.040
You're wrong.

811
01:00:47.910 --> 01:00:51.710
Sophie's mother ran away from her when she was a little girl.

812
01:00:55.084 --> 01:00:58.212
It's different, Gran. It's different.

813
01:01:03.259 --> 01:01:04.749
You're right.

814
01:01:06.229 --> 01:01:07.355
But

815
01:01:11.234 --> 01:01:13.896
Sophie's mother chose to leave her.

816
01:01:14.904 --> 01:01:17.737
And you always knew your mum and dad loved you.

817
01:01:38.728 --> 01:01:39.990
Come in.

818
01:01:44.600 --> 01:01:45.624
Hi.

819
01:01:57.280 --> 01:01:58.611
Oh, dear.

820
01:02:03.753 --> 01:02:06.551
I just wanted to see if you were all right.

821
01:02:06.622 --> 01:02:07.850
I'm okay.

822
01:02:15.965 --> 01:02:18.433
Claire, I'm so sorry if I caused you...

823
01:02:18.501 --> 01:02:19.593
No.

824
01:02:20.002 --> 01:02:22.800
Every minute has been so special.

825
01:02:27.944 --> 01:02:29.309
Darling.

826
01:02:52.168 --> 01:02:53.362
May I?

827
01:03:03.146 --> 01:03:07.480
One of the great joys in life is having one's hair brushed.

828
01:03:23.866 --> 01:03:25.925
Charlie, say you're sorry.

829
01:03:28.104 --> 01:03:31.733
In the eye, Charlie. With contrition.

830
01:03:39.148 --> 01:03:40.445
Sophie...

831
01:03:41.117 --> 01:03:42.209
Thank you. ... I'm afraid I...

832
01:03:42.285 --> 01:03:43.684
In the eye.

833
01:03:44.487 --> 01:03:46.387
Yes, Gran, I'm not 12.

834
01:03:48.391 --> 01:03:49.619
Prove it.

835
01:03:58.734 --> 01:04:01.669
I'm sincerely sorry, Sophie.

836
01:04:06.442 --> 01:04:08.000
It won't happen again.

837
01:04:08.945 --> 01:04:10.276
Excellent.

838
01:04:13.382 --> 01:04:14.542
Sophie,

839
01:04:15.051 --> 01:04:17.542
I want to make a toast. Thank you.

840
01:04:17.920 --> 01:04:22.127
I want to thank you for the wonderful thing you did

841
01:04:22.400 --> 01:04:23.325
for a complete stranger.

842
01:04:23.392 --> 01:04:25.223
I'll never forget it.

843
01:04:25.728 --> 01:04:26.752
Nor I.

844
01:04:26.829 --> 01:04:31.027
I've had the most incredible few days. I really have,

845
01:04:31.934 --> 01:04:34.164
and I can't thank you enough for letting me tag along.

846
01:04:34.237 --> 01:04:37.104
I think you did more than tag along.

847
01:04:37.640 --> 01:04:41.940
Here is to all our loved ones, wherever they may be.

848
01:05:01.797 --> 01:05:03.287
May I join you?

849
01:05:03.366 --> 01:05:04.594
Please.

850
01:05:15.344 --> 01:05:17.073
I am sorry, Sophie.

851
01:05:24.787 --> 01:05:28.621
I used to lay out under the stars as a kid, in the summertime.

852
01:05:31.560 --> 01:05:34.825
"Doubt thou the stars are fire

853
01:05:35.331 --> 01:05:37.561
"Doubt that the sun doth move"

854
01:05:39.101 --> 01:05:41.001
"Doubt truth be a liar

855
01:05:42.038 --> 01:05:44.006
"But never doubt I love"

856
01:05:51.547 --> 01:05:54.277
Do you think he's out there? Lorenzo?

857
01:05:54.583 --> 01:05:55.709
Yeah.

858
01:05:57.520 --> 01:05:59.044
He's somewhere.

859
01:06:01.791 --> 01:06:04.282
And am I wrong? To insist she quit?

860
01:06:05.795 --> 01:06:07.262
No, you know,

861
01:06:09.165 --> 01:06:11.099
you can't go on forever.

862
01:06:11.534 --> 01:06:13.832
At some point, you have to stop.

863
01:06:16.005 --> 01:06:19.372
Then why is it that I feel like a schoolboy on Sunday?

864
01:06:23.079 --> 01:06:25.604
It's nearly tomorrow and I don't want to go.

865
01:06:27.850 --> 01:06:29.249
Me, either.

866
01:06:30.953 --> 01:06:33.012
And I always liked school.

867
01:07:41.457 --> 01:07:43.118
Morning. Morning.

868
01:07:43.492 --> 01:07:44.550
Thank you.

869
01:07:44.627 --> 01:07:45.924
How'd you sleep?

870
01:07:45.995 --> 01:07:48.020
I slept fine. How about you?

871
01:07:48.464 --> 01:07:50.659
Yes. Great. Thanks.

872
01:07:55.671 --> 01:07:57.639
Good morning. Good morning.

873
01:07:58.441 --> 01:08:00.534
How are you? Good morning, my darling.

874
01:08:01.377 --> 01:08:03.208
It's a beautiful day.

875
01:08:07.383 --> 01:08:10.009
Sophie, would you mind going in the front?

876
01:08:10.010 --> 01:08:12.686
Because I'd love to stretch my legs for a bit.

877
01:08:15.224 --> 01:08:16.316
Okay.

878
01:08:18.961 --> 01:08:20.656
Fantastic, darling.

879
01:08:24.133 --> 01:08:26.931
The stars were spectacular from my window.

880
01:08:45.221 --> 01:08:49.749
Look. That vineyard. That's our favorite wine.

881
01:08:49.825 --> 01:08:54.125
Let's turn in there and have a goodbye toast.

882
01:08:54.196 --> 01:08:55.288
Okay.

883
01:09:17.620 --> 01:09:19.178
Charlie, stop!

884
01:09:35.137 --> 01:09:36.764
Nana, what is it?

885
01:09:39.608 --> 01:09:41.075
It's Lorenzo.

886
01:09:43.312 --> 01:09:44.404
Who?

887
01:09:46.549 --> 01:09:47.777
The boy?

888
01:09:51.253 --> 01:09:54.848
All right, Nana. Let's get you in the shade. Shall we?

889
01:10:06.635 --> 01:10:08.500
I am Lorenzo Bartolini.

890
01:10:12.741 --> 01:10:14.675
Sophie. I think this is...

891
01:10:15.044 --> 01:10:17.774
Do you know a Lorenzo Bartolini? I asked him and...

892
01:10:17.846 --> 01:10:20.246
I am Lorenzo Bartolini. You are!

893
01:10:21.217 --> 01:10:23.742
Oh, my... Sophie, he's the grandson!

894
01:10:26.055 --> 01:10:27.249
Can I help you? Yes.

895
01:10:27.323 --> 01:10:29.883
We're looking for a Lorenzo Bartolini.

896
01:10:29.959 --> 01:10:31.153
I am Lorenzo Bartolini.

897
01:10:31.227 --> 01:10:34.424
He's Lorenzo, too. Do you have a father also named Lorenzo?

898
01:10:34.496 --> 01:10:37.158
Yes, he went out riding. But what can I do for you?

899
01:10:38.267 --> 01:10:40.735
I'm sorry. We're being terribly rude.

900
01:10:40.803 --> 01:10:42.634
I'm Charlie Wyman. I'm Sophie.

901
01:10:42.705 --> 01:10:44.070
Lorenzo. Lorenzo.

902
01:10:44.139 --> 01:10:47.108
We have been on quite a journey for your father.

903
01:10:47.176 --> 01:10:49.770
You see, my grandmother, Claire, knows him.

904
01:10:50.145 --> 01:10:51.874
Charlie. Charlie, let's go!

905
01:10:53.349 --> 01:10:54.577
Let's go!

906
01:10:55.651 --> 01:10:57.050
All right.

907
01:11:01.290 --> 01:11:04.282
Nana, Nana, Nana, Lorenzo is here.

908
01:11:04.693 --> 01:11:07.457
He's out riding, but he's going to be back any minute.

909
01:11:08.998 --> 01:11:11.933
Well, then, let's go before he comes back.

910
01:11:12.001 --> 01:11:14.231
Nana, we've come all this way.

911
01:11:14.837 --> 01:11:16.964
Come on. I've been ridiculous.

912
01:11:17.039 --> 01:11:19.303
I've been completely ridiculous.

913
01:11:19.375 --> 01:11:23.744
He knew me when I was 15 years old, a girl.

914
01:11:25.414 --> 01:11:27.006
That girl's gone.

915
01:11:33.722 --> 01:11:35.314
Oh, my God.

916
01:12:39.488 --> 01:12:40.682
Claire?

917
01:12:44.593 --> 01:12:45.685
Yes.

918
01:12:53.001 --> 01:12:54.491
So many years.

919
01:12:54.970 --> 01:12:56.801
Yes, 50.

920
01:13:51.493 --> 01:13:53.085
If you allow me.

921
01:13:54.997 --> 01:13:57.830
I really don't know how to express myself.

922
01:13:59.668 --> 01:14:03.627
Well, I went for a ride this morning as an old man,

923
01:14:04.573 --> 01:14:07.098
and I came back as a teenager again.

924
01:14:08.410 --> 01:14:11.277
I'm so very happy you all meet Claire

925
01:14:12.147 --> 01:14:14.172
and her grandson, Charlie,

926
01:14:15.017 --> 01:14:17.349
and their lovely friend, Sophie.

927
01:14:21.356 --> 01:14:22.880
My dear Claire,

928
01:14:25.194 --> 01:14:27.719
you have told me you lost your Jack.

929
01:14:29.131 --> 01:14:31.725
And you know I lost my Rosa. Yeah.

930
01:14:33.068 --> 01:14:35.593
But destiny wanted us to meet again.

931
01:14:42.010 --> 01:14:44.308
Health to the Bartolini family.

932
01:14:45.781 --> 01:14:46.907
To the Bartolinis.

933
01:14:54.990 --> 01:14:56.787
I'm sorry I was late.

934
01:14:58.460 --> 01:14:59.484
No.

935
01:15:02.164 --> 01:15:05.531
When we are speaking about love, it's never too late.

936
01:15:24.553 --> 01:15:26.282
Well, bad luck, eh?

937
01:15:27.489 --> 01:15:31.550
All hope lost and he rides in at the last moment on a white horse.

938
01:15:32.227 --> 01:15:34.127
No one will believe it.

939
01:15:35.230 --> 01:15:36.697
Promise me...

940
01:15:37.833 --> 01:15:40.324
Promise me you'll finish the story.

941
01:15:47.943 --> 01:15:49.672
I guess this is it.

942
01:15:52.848 --> 01:15:54.611
And I'm sorry it is.

943
01:15:56.718 --> 01:15:58.185
Yeah, me, too.

944
01:16:04.192 --> 01:16:05.591
Sophie, I...

945
01:16:09.865 --> 01:16:13.926
I never should've kissed you. I was completely out of bounds.

946
01:16:18.540 --> 01:16:20.337
You're engaged and...

947
01:16:36.491 --> 01:16:38.083
It's time to go.

948
01:16:44.433 --> 01:16:46.060
Goodbye, Charlie.

949
01:16:48.136 --> 01:16:49.626
I'll miss you.

950
01:16:57.179 --> 01:16:59.773
Hi. It is time to...

951
01:17:00.782 --> 01:17:03.717
I must get back to Victor. Sorry.

952
01:17:05.087 --> 01:17:06.611
Yes. Of course.

953
01:17:06.688 --> 01:17:09.714
So, where's Charlie? We'll give you a drive there straight away.

954
01:17:09.791 --> 01:17:11.122
No, I'm...

955
01:17:11.793 --> 01:17:14.159
Claire, I can't take you away from this place, so...

956
01:17:14.229 --> 01:17:16.026
My son can drive you.

957
01:17:18.266 --> 01:17:20.029
Just a moment. Yes. Grazie.

958
01:17:28.744 --> 01:17:30.541
I'm so happy for you.

959
01:17:34.583 --> 01:17:36.278
Is something wrong?

960
01:17:36.351 --> 01:17:37.545
No! No.

961
01:17:38.954 --> 01:17:40.979
No. It's just, you know...

962
01:17:43.792 --> 01:17:45.521
And you're leaving?

963
01:17:46.795 --> 01:17:48.888
An angel brought you to me.

964
01:17:50.732 --> 01:17:52.063
Thank you.

965
01:17:53.869 --> 01:17:56.804
Thank you. I'll be eternally grateful.

966
01:18:01.276 --> 01:18:02.607
Thank you.

967
01:18:14.222 --> 01:18:16.247
It's very kind. Thank you.

968
01:18:17.526 --> 01:18:19.187
Thank you so much.

969
01:18:20.929 --> 01:18:23.625
You're always welcome here. Thank you.

970
01:18:24.533 --> 01:18:26.467
Thank you, Lorenzo. Ciao.

971
01:18:26.535 --> 01:18:28.059
Speak soon.

972
01:18:30.772 --> 01:18:31.830
Bye.

973
01:18:58.700 --> 01:19:02.033
Darling, you're family,

974
01:19:02.104 --> 01:19:07.098
so I think you'll understand why I'm telling you you're a total idiot.

975
01:19:08.076 --> 01:19:10.806
Okay, okay, then don't sugarcoat it, Gran.

976
01:19:10.879 --> 01:19:12.107
I won't.

977
01:19:12.180 --> 01:19:15.638
How many Sophies do you think there are on this planet?

978
01:19:19.054 --> 01:19:21.352
Don't wait 50 years like I did.

979
01:19:24.993 --> 01:19:27.655
Go. Go! Go!

980
01:20:18.713 --> 01:20:21.443
I missed you so much! So much.

981
01:20:43.805 --> 01:20:46.307
...in person because I couldn't send you an e-mail,

982
01:20:46.308 --> 01:20:48.742
Because it was... It just didn't make sense,

983
01:20:48.743 --> 01:20:50.945
because I know you were having...

984
01:20:52.147 --> 01:20:53.512
One second.

985
01:20:53.915 --> 01:20:56.076
Hello? Hey. Yeah, yeah.

986
01:20:57.352 --> 01:20:59.978
We just have to sort out all the deliveries that we need to make.

987
01:20:59.979 --> 01:21:00.880
I mean...

988
01:21:38.159 --> 01:21:39.558
What happened?

989
01:21:40.028 --> 01:21:41.757
You don't like the ending?

990
01:21:41.830 --> 01:21:44.094
No, the ending's fine. It's actually quite moving.

991
01:21:44.165 --> 01:21:46.895
But what happened to the people? I mean, are they still together?

992
01:21:46.968 --> 01:21:50.199
What about... What about the kid? What about Charlie? Where's he?

993
01:21:51.306 --> 01:21:53.900
I don't know. I didn't keep in touch.

994
01:21:57.345 --> 01:21:59.245
Do you have any advice?

995
01:22:00.282 --> 01:22:03.080
I think you should buy stock in Alitalia,

996
01:22:04.085 --> 01:22:07.179
'cause all the ladies are gonna be flocking to Verona.

997
01:22:07.822 --> 01:22:09.153
Are you gonna publish this?

998
01:22:09.224 --> 01:22:12.216
Yeah, I'm gonna publish it. It's a good story.

999
01:22:12.694 --> 01:22:14.321
You got anything else for me?

1000
01:22:15.063 --> 01:22:16.928
No, not at the moment.

1001
01:22:23.104 --> 01:22:24.469
Oh, my God.

1002
01:22:29.511 --> 01:22:31.979
You said Tuscan gold, right? Tuscan gold?

1003
01:22:32.580 --> 01:22:34.138
Look. This is yellow.

1004
01:22:34.215 --> 01:22:35.549
Yellow. Tuscan gold. Yellow.

1005
01:22:35.550 --> 01:22:36.912
I mean, there's no Tuscany, there's no gold.

1006
01:22:36.985 --> 01:22:38.850
It's just like Limoncello, you know. Sure.

1007
01:22:38.920 --> 01:22:40.251
Yeah? Can we do it again? Yeah. Yeah.

1008
01:22:40.322 --> 01:22:42.051
Yeah? All right, all right, all right. Let's go. Come on.

1009
01:22:42.123 --> 01:22:43.852
Let's try it again.

1010
01:22:44.492 --> 01:22:46.119
Hey, Sophie. Hi.

1011
01:22:46.761 --> 01:22:48.752
Hey, how's your day been?

1012
01:22:49.364 --> 01:22:52.299
I showed my story to Bobby. Did he like it?

1013
01:22:53.134 --> 01:22:55.102
Yeah, he's gonna print it.

1014
01:22:55.170 --> 01:22:56.432
Print it? Yeah.

1015
01:22:56.504 --> 01:22:58.131
Wow! Wow. That's pretty great, huh?

1016
01:22:58.206 --> 01:22:59.935
I know. Did you read it? Yeah, yeah.

1017
01:23:00.008 --> 01:23:03.239
No. I... Last night I left it out for you to read.

1018
01:23:03.311 --> 01:23:06.712
I don't need to. I mean, I know it's gonna be great.

1019
01:23:06.781 --> 01:23:08.772
Plus, I'm gonna see it... I'm gonna see it when it comes out.

1020
01:23:08.850 --> 01:23:09.874
That's why I prefer to buy it.

1021
01:23:09.951 --> 01:23:11.282
You know, I'm surprised, like... Yeah.

1022
01:23:11.353 --> 01:23:13.150
You know? Like... Watch out! Watch out!

1023
01:23:14.789 --> 01:23:15.847
Hold on, hold on, hold on.

1024
01:23:29.971 --> 01:23:32.439
Thanks for lunch. Oh, my God. You so deserve this.

1025
01:23:32.507 --> 01:23:33.906
I want to be your fact checker for life.

1026
01:23:33.975 --> 01:23:36.000
Don't ever think about going anywhere else, ever. Ever.

1027
01:23:36.077 --> 01:23:37.840
I promise.

1028
01:23:37.912 --> 01:23:39.607
Hi, guys. Hey.

1029
01:23:39.681 --> 01:23:42.172
Sophie, these messages are for you.

1030
01:23:43.585 --> 01:23:46.076
